  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the alert rule. The rule is enabled and has already run once with status `ok`, so the query runs without errors. I haven't seen it fire yet.\n\n**1. Data exploration.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view has a time field, `timestamp`, and one document per web request. The fields that matter most are:\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as a string, with a `.keyword` version\n- `bytes`\n- `clientip`\n- `url`, `request` and `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `agent` and `referer`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-3c6e32be Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-3c6e32be-web-logs-overview-108fbbb4fbef`) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique visitors (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Total bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time, split by response code (stacked bar)\n- Share of each response code (pie)\n- Top requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table)\n- Top source countries (bar on `geo.src`)\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-3c6e32be 5xx spike\"** (id `e2e0e812-5c98-467e-90a2-2c00ba0968b3`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within a 5-minute window, using `timestamp` as the time field. A request counts as 5xx when `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\". That text comparison works because the status codes are always three digits.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it will show up in Kibana but won't send notifications anywhere. To get alerts by email or Slack, you'd need to add a connector to it.",
